        "id": 1002751
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-03-18T22:22:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since we don\u0027t particularly care which file the symlink points to, would it be reasonable to do this as a side-effect of the rule that copies to the symbols directory, and just let the latest one win?  It could be made atomic by creating the symlink with a unique suffix and then renaming that into the final location.",
      "revId": "5e5f2105d4470102d33c2968454f25f669d8238b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d8339288_70c54662",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 17,
      "author": {
        "id": 1067098
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-03-18T23:39:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are two updates that need to be done when updating a file in the symbols directory.\n1. We need to create a symlink for the new build-id.\n2. We need to delete the symlink for the old build-id, or make it refer to another existing symbols file with the old build-id if it exists. Without that, if I make a change that causes build-ids to diverge (i.e. two binaries previously having the same build-id now have different build-ids), we may be unable to symbolize using the old build-id because the symlink could point to the wrong file (i.e. the one whose build-id changed).\n\n1 is easy as long as we\u0027re willing to accept the non-determinism that you mentioned. 2 is tricky because we don\u0027t know how many references from other symbol files are still in use (at least I couldn\u0027t see a good non-racy way to do it).",
      "parentUuid": "4197ed04_e19a594f",
      "revId": "5e5f2105d4470102d33c2968454f25f669d8238b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
